<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Shishman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Šišman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Shishman was the last tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, reigning from 1371 to 1395. A son of Ivan Alexander, he inherited a divided realm and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>. Tarnovo fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1393, and he was captured and executed in 1395. Popular tradition made him a hero of resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman.jpg" alt="Ivan Shishman" width="148" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:53_IoSisiman.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Shishman was born in 1350 or 1351, the eldest son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</a> and his second wife Sarah-Theodora, a Jewish convert to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>. Ivan Alexander's eldest son by his first marriage, Michael Asen IV, died in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1355, and the succession was settled in Ivan Shishman's favour because he had been born after his father's coronation. By the end of 1355 he had been proclaimed heir and co-emperor. His half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a> was given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, probably as compensation, and in 1356 proclaimed himself emperor there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ivan Shishman was proclaimed emperor on 17 February 1371, when he was in his early twenties. He inherited only part of his father's realm, the lands between the Iskar River and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silistra">Silistra</a>, the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodope-mountains">Rhodope Mountains</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Ivan Sratsimir ruled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>, and the Principality of Karvuna under the despot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa</a> also refused to recognise him. After the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> on 26 September 1371, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> conquered northern Thrace and the Rhodopes. In 1373 Ivan Shishman became an Ottoman vassal and gave his sister Kera Tamara in marriage to Murad, regaining some towns. Sofia fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1385.</p><p>After the victory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> at the Battle of Pločnik in 1387, he renounced his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. An Ottoman army of 30,000 under the Grand Vizier Çandarlızade Ali Pasha took Shumen and other fortresses in 1388 and besieged him at Nikopol, and he had to accept harsher terms, including Ottoman garrisons in several towns. In the spring of 1393 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Sultan Bayezid I</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, whose defence was led by Patriarch Evtimiy while the emperor was at Nikopol. The city fell on 17 July 1393. According to the cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-tsamblak">Gregory Tsamblak</a>, it fell through treason. Ivan Shishman's lands were reduced to Nikopol and a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bayezid captured Nikopol after returning from Wallachia in 1395. According to the Anonymous Bulgarian Chronicle, Ivan Shishman was killed there, a Byzantine chronicle gives a different date, and some sources suggest he died in prison. Bulgarian historians have often judged him harshly, as a ruler who took the throne through his mother's intrigues and divided the country on the eve of the invasion. Under him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> remained a cultural centre, where Patriarch Evtimiy carried out an orthographic reform.</p><p>In Bulgarian folklore he is the most prominent medieval ruler, remembered as a hero who fought and died against overwhelming forces, and many fortresses, caves and places are named after him. Rebels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a> in 1598 proclaimed a claimed descendant as Shishman III.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Shishman of Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Basarab I's victory at the Battle of Posada in 1330 won independence from Charles I of Hungary; the first clash with the Ottomans came under Vladislav I, allied with <b>Ivan Shishman</b> of Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal charters granting land and privileges to monasteries, among them <b>Ivan Shishman</b>'s charter to the Rila Monastery of 1378, survive as rare documents of Bulgarian administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Tsar Ivan Alexander of the Second Bulgarian Empire made his younger son <b>Ivan Shishman</b> heir and gave his eldest, Ivan Sratsimir, the north-western region around Vidin.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsardom-of-vidin">Tsardom of Vidin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in Nikopol, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1385" aria-label="Veliko Tarnovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 634 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ivan Shishman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5618 904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5618 904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1385">What was here in 1385? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
